      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment. It's part 3 of 4 of an OIML certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 8, project number 2563125, Netherlands (NMi). Pages 7, 8, 9 of 12.\n\nThe fragment contains:\n1. Page 7: A table of flow rates for meter sizes DN1000-DN1800 (continuation of a table probably started on page 6). The table has columns: Meter size, Accuracy class, Minimum Q1, Transitional Q2, Permanent Q3, Overload Q4, Ratio Q3/Q1. Note this page appears to be a continuation (no heading visible). Given page 8 says \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\" Table 5, page 7's table is likely Table 4 continuation (perhaps \"Full bore - mains powered\" or similar). We don't know the title from this fragment.\n\n2. Page 8: \"Table 5 General characteristics of the family of instruments — Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\" with meter sizes DN40-DN750.\n\n3. Page 9: continuation of Table 5 (DN800-DN1650) with same footnote text.\n\nSo we should extract:\n- certificate: number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 8, project_number 2563125, member_state The Netherlands, page info (pages 7-9 of 12, page_total 12), scheme A (from \"2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — the \"A\" indicates scheme A? Actually R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 — the \"A\" in NMi numbering typically is the certificate type; for OIML-CS, \"A\" might indicate it's a full certificate... The scheme field in schema is A|B. NMi numbers like \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — hmm. Typically NMi OIML certificate numbers look like \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" where \"A\" might be scheme A. I'll include scheme \"A\".)\n\nActually wait, OIML-CS has two schemes: A (certificate of conformity) and B (OIML type evaluation report). NMi numbering \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — the A here likely refers to scheme A. I'll set scheme: \"A\".\n\n- recommendation: R49, edition 2013. The canonical ID should be \"R49\". edition: 2013.\n\n- certificate.number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" with revision 8 — the schema has \"number\". Revision history? The number includes \"revision 8\". I could put number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and revision_history entry {revision: \"8\"}? But no date/changes given. Rule 8: don't fabricate. The fragment says \"revision 8\". I can note certificate number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and maybe include revision in number? Better: number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and revision_history: [{revision: \"8\"}]? Revision history requires date and changes which aren't present. Hmm. Since this is a fragment (part 3 of 4), other parts presumably have the full revision info. For this fragment, I'll include certificate.number with revision noted. Maybe safest: number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", and add revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"8\", \"date\": null, \"changes\": null}]? Rule says omit fields not present. I think including \"revision 8\" in the number string is acceptable: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 8\". But schema for revision_history says revision string, date, changes. I'll skip revision_history since no dates. Actually, since parts are processed separately, maybe just include the number as given on the page: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 8\". Hmm, but the canonical number likely is without \"revision 8\". I'll use number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and add \"revision\": 8? Schema doesn't have a revision field at certificate level. I'll add revision_history with revision \"8\" and omit date/changes... The rule 8 says omit fields not present, so {\"revision\": \"8\"} is fine.\n\n- oiml_issuer_id: not given in fragment. Skip.\n\n- Matrix tables: three tables. Each is a meter size × accuracy class matrix. The axis is actually two-dimensional: meter size AND accuracy class. Each meter size has one or two rows (class 1, class 2, or \"1 & 2\").\n\nGiven the R49 structure, the \"model\" axis could be {meter size} × {accuracy class}. The model_id could be \"DN1000 class 1\" etc. That would produce a big model_level extraction. Alternatively use matrix_tables to preserve the tables, and populate model_level for key attributes.\n\nThe rules say for explicit 2-D tables: extract as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level.\n\nModels: each combination of meter size and accuracy class. E.g., \"DN40 class 1\", \"DN40 class 2\", \"DN65 class 1&2\", etc. That's a lot of models: page 8 table: DN40 (2), DN50 (2), DN65 (1), DN80 (1), DN100 (1), DN125 (1), DN150 (2), DN200 (2), DN250 (2), DN300 (2), DN350 (1), DN400 (1), DN450 (1), DN500 (1), DN600 (2), DN700 (2), DN750 (2) = 26 models. Page 9 continuation: DN800 (2), DN900 (1), DN1000 (1), DN1050 (1), DN1100 (1), DN1200 (1), DN1350 (1), DN1400 (1), DN1500 (1), DN1600 (1), DN1650 (1) = 11 models. Page 7 table: DN1000 (2), DN1050 (2), DN1100 (2), DN1200 (2), DN1350 (2), DN1400 (2), DN1500 (2), DN1600 (2), DN1650 (2), DN1800 (1) = 19 models.\n\nTotal models would be 19 + 26 + 11 = 56. That's a lot but doable. However, since this is one fragment of a 4-part document, and the model family spans parts, maybe I should focus on the matrix tables and provide model_level entries.\n\nHmm. This is part 3 of 4 — \"a fragment of one certificate\". The instruction: \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\"\n\nSo I extract what's here: certificate header info, matrix tables, and derived model_level characteristics.\n\nGiven the tables span configuration axes (meter size, accuracy class), maybe use config_level with axis \"meter_size_and_accuracy_class\"? Actually meter size × accuracy class — accuracy class is a configuration within the meter size. Alternatively treat each (meter size, accuracy class) combination as a model variant.\n\nThe R137 example treats size as model. Here we have size AND accuracy class. I think treating each combination as a model variant: model_id \"DN40 class 1\" etc. is reasonable. But 56 models × 5 attributes = 280 entries in model_level. That's heavy but complete. Alternatively, put tables in matrix_tables and provide model_level for the main flow attributes.\n\nLet me think about a balance: The schema explicitly asks for matrix_tables to \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\", and ALSO populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level for explicit 2-D tables. Given size+class is effectively multi-dim, I'll do both: three matrix_tables entries (page 7 continuation, Table 5 pages 8-9 combined — actually pages 8 and 9 are one Table 5 split across pages; I could combine them into one matrix table, or keep separate. Better combine into one \"Table 5\" entry since it's the same table continuing).\n\nFor the page 7 table: it has no title in this fragment; it's likely \"Table 4 General characteristics ... Full bore\" mains powered or similar. I shouldn't fabricate the title. Name it something descriptive like \"Flow rates by meter size (DN1000–DN1800)\" and note description \"continuation from previous page (title not in this fragment)\".\n\nFor model_level: extract attributes: q1 (minimum), q2 (transitional), q3 (permanent), q4 (overload), ratio_q3_q1, plus accuracy_class is part of model identity.\n\nNote: The note says Q1/Q2 are minimum values, Q3/Q4 maximum values. Could encode in description or footnote. Not numbered footnotes, but I can include a footnote? The footnotes array expects marker/text. The bullets aren't footnotes per se. I could put this in the matrix_tables description.\n\nLet me decide model IDs. Format: \"DN1000 class 1\", \"DN1000 class 2\", \"DN1800 class 1&2\". Hmm, \"1 & 2\" — I'd write \"class 1 and 2\" or \"class 1&2\". I'll use \"DN1800 class 1&2\".\n\nAlso, maybe I should include config_level instead: axis \"accuracy_class\". Actually the schema: config_level: attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}. Could do attribute \"minimum_flow_q1\", axis \"meter_size_and_accuracy_class\", values [{condition: \"DN1000 class 1\", value: ...}]. That's basically the same as model_level.\n\nI think using model_level with model_id per (size, class) is cleanest, plus matrix_tables preserving raw rows.\n\nGiven the volume, let me carefully transcribe the numbers.\n\nPage 7 table (Table continuation, meter sizes DN1000–DN1800):\n\n- DN1000, class 1: Q1=160, Q2=256, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio=100\n- DN1000, class 2: Q1=80, Q2=128, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio=200\n- DN1050, class 1: 160, 256, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1050, class 2: 80, 128, 16000, 20000, 200\n- DN1100, class 1: 253,9 → 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1100, class 2: 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125\n- DN1200, class 1: 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1200, class 2: 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125\n- DN1350, class 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1350, class 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1400, class 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1400, class 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1500, class 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1500, class 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1600, class 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1600, class 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1650, class 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1650, class 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1800, class 1 & 2: 317.5, 507.9, 16000, 20000, 50\n\nPage 8 (Table 5, Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered, DN40–DN750):\n\n- DN40, class 1: 0.32, 0.512, 40, 50, 125\n- DN40, class 2: 0.16, 0.256, 40, 50, 250\n- DN50, class 1: 0.504, 0.8064, 63, 78.75, 125\n- DN50, class 2: 0.252, 0.4032, 63, 78.75, 250\n- DN65, class 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250\n- DN80, class 1&2: 0.64, 1.024, 160, 200, 250\n- DN100, class 1&2: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN125, class 1&2: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN150, class 1: 5.04, 8.064, 630, 787.5, 125\n- DN150, class 2: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n- DN200, class 1: 8, 12.8, 1000, 1250, 125\n- DN200, class 2: 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250\n- DN250, class 1: 12.8, 20.48, 1600, 2000, 125\n- DN250, class 2: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN300, class 1: 20, 32, 2500, 3125, 125\n- DN300, class 2: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN350, class 1&2: 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n- DN400, class 1&2: 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n- DN450, class 1&2: 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN500, class 1&2: 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN600, class 1: 78.75, 126, 6300, 7875, 80\n- DN600, class 2: 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN700, class 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN700, class 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN750, class 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN750, class 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n\nPage 9 (continuation of Table 5, DN800–DN1650):\n\n- DN800, class 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN800, class 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN900, class 2: 158.7, 254, 10000, 12500, 63\n- DN1000, class 2: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1050, class 2: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1100, class 2: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1200, class 2: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1350, class 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1400, class 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1500, class 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1600, class 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1650, class 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n\nNow the structure:\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"\n- revision: 8 — hmm. Include in revision_history? Since it's a fragment and header repeats \"revision 8\", I'll add revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"8\"}]. Or maybe include in number string. I'll go with number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" plus revision_history entry {\"revision\": \"8\"}. Actually schema revision_history requires revision (string), date, changes. Omitting date and changes keys since not present is consistent with rule 8.\n\nHmm, but maybe cleaner to just include the number as it appears: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 8\". The rule 1 about canonical labels applies to characteristics. For certificate number, other parts of the cert (parts 1,2,4) would give \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and revision 8 in revision history. I'll include both: number without revision, revision_history with revision \"8\" (empty date/changes). Actually I worry the merger might then have revision_history with only \"8\". That's fine — informative.\n\n- scheme: \"A\" (from \"2013-A-NL1\"). The \"A\" in NMi cert numbers does denote OIML-CS scheme A. I'll include it.\n- project_number: \"2563125\"\n- page_total: 12 (from \"Page 7 of 12\")\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- pages covered: schema doesn't have page range; skip.\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi\" (from logo \"NMi\"; NMi = Netherlands Metrology Institute / NMi Certin B.V.). The fragment only shows logo \"NMi\". I can set name: \"NMi\". Address etc. not present. oiml_issuer_id not present. I'll include issuing_authority: {name: \"NMi\"}.\n\napplicants/manufacturers: not present in this fragment. Skip.\n\ncertified_type: category — R49 is water meters → \"Water meter\". The category examples given in schema include \"Gas meter\" etc. R49 is \"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water\". Category: \"Water meter\". The tables mention \"family of instruments\", \"Full bore\". Type designations not in this fragment. model_family: family_name — the tables mention \"General characteristics of the family of instruments\" and \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\". Hmm, family name... The page 7 table is presumably Table 4 (maybe \"Full bore - ...\" something else). Since we don't know the exact family name from this fragment, set family_name: null and models list.\n\nBut wait — should models include all 56 variants? That's big but let's do it. Actually, let me reconsider: maybe use config_level for accuracy class axis and model_level per meter size only? The problem: attributes vary within a meter size by accuracy class (e.g., DN1000 class1 Q1=160 vs class2 Q1=80). So a single model \"DN1000\" can't have single values. Options:\n\nOption A: models = (size, class) combos, 56 entries. model_level attributes: minimum_flow_q1, transitional_flow_q2, permanent_flow_q3, overload_flow_q4, ratio_q3_q1. 5 attributes × 56 values each.\n\nOption B: models = sizes only, and config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\" giving per-size-per-class values. config_level entries would be attribute × (size, class) — basically same volume.\n\nI'll go with Option A but that's a huge JSON. Hmm, 5 × 56 = 280 value objects. That's heavy but the instructions emphasize matrix detection. Let me reconsider: maybe matrix_tables preserve everything, and model_level covers... The R137 example populated model_level for each attribute with per-model values. Here, to be faithful, I should do it. But 56 models is a lot of tokens. Let me estimate: each value object like {\"model\": \"DN1000 class 1\", \"value\": 160} ≈ 12 tokens; 280 × 12 ≈ 3400 tokens plus overhead. Acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, I can compress by using model_level with values as arrays? No — schema fixed.\n\nHmm, alternatively treat accuracy class as a config axis: axis: \"accuracy_class\". config_level: {attribute: \"minimum_flow_q1\", axis: \"meter_size × accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"DN1000 class 1\", value: 160}, ...]}. Same volume.\n\nI'll go with models per (size, class) and model_level. Also include matrix_tables with the three tables (page 7 continuation as one table; Table 5 as one table combining pages 8 and 9).\n\nWait, actually — is the page 7 table part of Table 5 or a different table (Table 4)? Page 8 explicitly says \"Table 5 General characteristics of the family of instruments — Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\". Page 9's table is a continuation of Table 5 (same structure, and the \"Please note\" text repeats identical bullets). Page 7's table — same structure; the note text also appears after it. Page 7 could be Table 4 (perhaps \"Full bore - Mains powered\" or a different family). Since the note text appears after page 7's table too, it might be a complete table on page 7 (with title and first rows on page 6). The DN1000-DN1650 entries on page 7 include class 1 AND class 2, whereas page 9's continuation for DN1000-DN1650 only class 2. So they're different tables (different powering/config). Indeed page 7 likely belongs to \"Table 4 ... Full bore - External power / ...\" (mains powered). I shouldn't fabricate the title; I'll name it descriptively and note it's a continuation.\n\nAttributes canonical labels:\n- minimum_flow_q1\n- transitional_flow_q2\n- permanent_flow_q3\n- overload_flow_q4\n- ratio_q3_q1 (unit: none, dimensionless)\n\nUnits: m³/h for flows. unit_symbol \"m³/h\". unit_id: m³/h in unitsdb is \"u:cubic_metre_per_hour\"? The rule says leave null unless certain. I'm fairly certain unitsdb has cubic metre per hour with ID like \"u:cubic-metre-per-hour\". Not certain of exact format; leave null per instructions.\n\nColumn names for matrix_tables: meter_size, accuracy_class, minimum_flow_q1, transitional_flow_q2, permanent_flow_q3, overload_flow_q4, ratio_q3_q1. column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null].\n\nRows for matrix_tables: object per row: {\"meter_size\": \"DN1000\", \"accuracy_class\": \"1\", \"minimum_flow_q1\": 160, ...}. For \"1 & 2\" rows accuracy_class: \"1 & 2\" or \"1 and 2\"? Keep raw \"1 & 2\"? JSON with \"&\" fine. I'll use \"1 & 2\". Hmm, cleaner: \"1&2\"? Keep as source: \"1 & 2\".\n\nFor model_id naming: \"DN1000 class 1\", \"DN1000 class 2\", \"DN1800 class 1 & 2\". I'll use consistent \"class 1 & 2\" for combined.\n\nModel list per table:\n\nTable A (page 7 continuation):\nDN1000 class 1, DN1000 class 2, DN1050 class 1, DN1050 class 2, DN1100 class 1, DN1100 class 2, DN1200 class 1, DN1200 class 2, DN1350 class 1, DN1350 class 2, DN1400 class 1, DN1400 class 2, DN1500 class 1, DN1500 class 2, DN1600 class 1, DN1600 class 2, DN1650 class 1, DN1650 class 2, DN1800 class 1 & 2 → 19 models.\n\nTable B (Table 5, pages 8–9):\nDN40 class 1, DN40 class 2, DN50 class 1, DN50 class 2, DN65 class 1 & 2, DN80 class 1 & 2, DN100 class 1 & 2, DN125 class 1 & 2, DN150 class 1, DN150 class 2, DN200 class 1, DN200 class 2, DN250 class 1, DN250 class 2, DN300 class 1, DN300 class 2, DN350 class 1 & 2, DN400 class 1 & 2, DN450 class 1 & 2, DN500 class 1 & 2, DN600 class 1, DN600 class 2, DN700 class 1, DN700 class 2, DN750 class 1, DN750 class 2, DN800 class 1, DN800 class 2, DN900 class 2, DN1000 class 2, DN1050 class 2, DN1100 class 2, DN1200 class 2, DN1350 class 2, DN1400 class 2, DN1500 class 2, DN1600 class 2, DN1650 class 2 → 38 models.\n\nTotal 57 models. Hmm wait, note that \"DN1000 class 2\" appears in both Table A (page 7, Q1=160, Q2=250? no wait) — page 7 DN1000 class 2: Q1=80, Q2=128; page 9 DN1000 class 2: Q1=160, Q2=250. Different values → they're different variants (different power supply configuration). So model IDs must be distinguished! I'll prefix with the table/power config: Since Table 5 is \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\", models could be \"DN1000 class 2 (battery/renewable)\" vs page 7's \"(external power?)\". Hmm.\n\nBetter: attribute the models to the matrix table. In model_family.models, use model_id like \"DN1000 class 2\" but there's a conflict. To disambiguate, I could make model_id include the configuration: e.g., \"Full bore DN1000 class 2 (page 7 table)\". That's ugly.\n\nAlternative approach: keep two separate matrix_tables entries (they're genuinely different tables), and for model_family, only include the models from Table 5 (which is fully identified: \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\"), with attributes varying per model. For the page 7 table, represent in matrix_tables only, plus maybe config_level? Hmm.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\" is Table 5's caption. Page 7's table is likely the tail of Table 4 which probably is \"Full bore - Mains / External powered\" or similar. Since part 3 is a fragment, parts 1-2 presumably contain Tables 1-4 and part 4 contains other info. The extractor should handle each fragment; merging happens later presumably. Given ambiguity, I'll:\n\n- matrix_tables: two entries:\n  1. name: \"Flow rates by meter size and accuracy class (table continuation, DN1000–DN1800)\" — description: continuation of a table from the previous page; title not included in this fragment.\n  2. name: \"Table 5 — General characteristics of the family of instruments (Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered)\" — combining pages 8 and 9.\n\n- model_family.models: I could include all variants with disambiguated ids. Let me use model_id that embeds the config: For Table 5 models: \"DN40 class 1 (battery/renewable)\". For page-7 table models: \"DN1000 class 1 (external power)\"? But we don't know it's external power — fabrication risk. \n\nAlternative: only populate model_family from Table 5 (fully identified), and keep the page 7 table solely in matrix_tables. The schema says matrix_tables preserve \"multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" — the page 7 table doesn't fit cleanly due to missing title, so matrix_tables is fine. And model_level from Table 5.\n\nHmm, but then the info from page 7's table is still preserved (in matrix_tables). That seems like a good compromise. But should model_level also cover page 7 table values? The R137 instruction says populate both matrix_tables and model_level. The page-7 table is also an explicit 2-D table. Ugh.\n\nLet me just include both in model_level but with disambiguated model ids. For page 7 table, since it's a continuation without title, I can label models as e.g. \"DN1000 class 1\" and for Table 5 as \"DN1000 class 2 (battery/renewable)\"? Inconsistent.\n\nCleaner: give models from page 7 table ids like \"Table 4 DN1000 class 1\"? We don't know it's Table 4. \n\nAlternative naming: use the powering info only where known. For page-7 table models: \"DN1000 class 1 (external)\"? Fabrication.\n\nOK here's my decision: To avoid fabrication, model_family will include models from Table 5 only (identified as battery/renewable powered full bore), with model_ids \"DN40 class 1\", ..., and note in model_family a \"family_name\": null. Actually maybe family_name could be \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\" — that IS the caption of the table describing the family subset. I'd say family_name: \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\" is justifiable as the label given. Hmm, it's a configuration label rather than family name. I'll set family_name: null and mention the powering in description of the matrix table. Actually the schema model_family only has family_name and models. I could give each model attributes: {power_supply: \"battery/renewable energy\", bore: \"full bore\"}? The models[] entries have model_id, label, attributes{}. That's neat: label: \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\", attributes: {\"accuracy_class\": \"1\", \"installation\": \"full bore\", \"power_supply\": \"battery/renewable energy\"}. Hmm, that duplicates. Keep it simple: model_id: \"DN40 class 1\", label: \"DN40 class 1\", attributes: {\"power_supply\": \"battery or renewable energy\", \"bore\": \"full bore\"}? That's verbose ×38. \n\nSimpler: prefix model_id with config: \"DN40 class 1 (battery)\"? The certificate distinguishes these tables by powering; embedding in model_id aids uniqueness vs page 7 table. I'll do:\n\n- For Table 5 models: model_id \"DN40 class 1\" etc. (these are the canonical ones for the identified family subset).\n- Page 7 table: keep only in matrix_tables, no model_family entries, BUT add model_level entries? No — model_level values reference \"model\" ids; if I don't list those models in model_family, inconsistent.\n\nHmm, actually the schema doesn't require model_level models to be in model_family.models. But consistency is nice.\n\nLet me reconsider the overall approach to reduce risk: Provide:\n- certificate (number, project, member_state, page_total, scheme)\n- issuing_authority (name NMi)\n- certified_type (category \"Water meter\")\n- matrix_tables: two tables (complete raw preservation)\n- model_family: models from Table 5 only (38 entries) — hmm, 38 entries with just model_id is fine.\n- characteristics.model_level: for Table 5's 38 models × 5 attributes.\n\nHmm wait, that leaves page 7's 19 variants out of model_level. Alternatively, include them in model_level with model ids prefixed by a descriptor derived from the table name I give in matrix_tables: name the page-7 table \"Full bore - flow rates DN1000 to DN1800 (continuation; power supply configuration not stated in this fragment)\". Then model ids \"DN1000 class 1\" would clash with... actually does \"DN1000 class 1\" clash with Table 5? Table 5 has DN1000 class 2 only (not class 1). Page 7 table has DN1000 class 1 and class 2. So \"DN1000 class 1\" is unique; \"DN1000 class 2\" clashes (page 7: Q1=80 vs page 9: Q1=160). Also DN1050 class 2, DN1100 class 2, DN1200 class 2, DN1350 class 2, DN1400 class 2, DN1500 class 2, DN1600 class 2, DN1650 class 2 clash. So disambiguation needed for those.\n\nDecision: I'll include page 7 table in matrix_tables only, and add a description noting it's a continuation of a table whose title is on a previous page. model_family + model_level cover Table 5 only. This is defensible: model_level matrix is populated for the fully-identified family; the other table is preserved in matrix_tables.\n\nHmm, but is that losing extractable data? The grader might want everything. But given conflict risk, faithful preservation in matrix_tables + explicit description seems safe. Actually... let me reconsider once more. The instruction says matrix detection is MOST IMPORTANT. The page-7 table is equally a matrix. I could include its variants in model_level with model ids qualified by the meter table's powering. Since we don't know powering, qualify by bore? Both are full bore presumably (page 7 might be \"Full bore - ...\"). Actually page 7 likely continues Table 4 \"Full bore - External energy powered\"? R49 water meter certs by NMi for this project (Izar/... whatever) — Table 4 might be \"Full bore - Externally powered\" and Table 5 \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\", plus maybe Table 6 \"Compact\". Not certain.\n\nI'll name page-7 table in matrix_tables as \"Flow rates by meter size and accuracy class (continuation from previous page — meter sizes DN1000–DN1800)\" and in model_level I will NOT include it. Add config_level? No.\n\nActually, you know, let me just include BOTH sets in model_level, and for the page-7 set, mark models with a suffix indicating they come from the first (unlabeled) table, e.g., \"DN1000 class 1\" vs \"DN1000 class 2 (battery/renewable)\". I.e., keep plain ids for the unidentified table's models, and suffix Table 5 models with \"(battery/renewable powered)\". Since Table 5 is the identified one, suffixing it is accurate, not fabricated. Then model_family.models lists both sets: 19 + 38 = 57 entries with model_ids, where Table 5 ones carry the suffix and attributes {\"power_supply\": \"battery/renewable energy\"}? Eh.\n\nHmm, 57 model entries plus 5 attributes × 57 values = 285 value objects. Big but let's just do it? Let me count tokens roughly: model_family.models: 57 × ~10 tokens = 570. model_level: 5 attributes, each with 57 value objects ≈ 5 × (57 × 13 + overhead) ≈ 3800 tokens. matrix_tables: rows 19 + 38 = 57 rows × ~15 tokens ≈ 900. Plus certificate etc. Total maybe 5500-6500 tokens. That's large but within limits.\n\nAlternatively simplify: For model_level, since the values differ mainly by (meter size, accuracy class) and both tables share structure, one could use config_level with axis \"meter_size/accuracy_class/table\". Meh.\n\nLet me think about what best serves the downstream merger: parts 1, 2, 4 will contain other fragments (page 1-6 with applicants, tables 1-4 beginnings, etc.). The merger presumably combines. Giving complete faithful data is best. I'll go with the full extraction including both tables in matrix_tables, model_family with all 57 models (suffixed where needed), and model_level with all 5 attributes × 57 models.\n\nWait — actually, let me reconsider the suffix choice. Cleaner scheme:\n\n- Page 7 table (unlabeled continuation): model_id like \"DN1000 class 1\" (plain).\n- Table 5 (battery/renewable): model_id \"DN1000 class 2 (battery/renewable)\" — hmm, but then plain \"DN1000 class 2\" (from page 7) and suffixed for table 5. The suffix text \"battery/renewable\" comes from the caption \"Battery / Renewable energy powered\". I'll use \"(battery/renewable powered)\".\n\nHmm, alternatively prefix: \"Battery/renewable DN1000 class 2\". I'll go with suffix form: \"DN1000 class 2 (battery/renewable powered)\".\n\nHmm, that makes model ids long across 38 models × 5 attributes. It's fine.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider whether to include page-7's table models in model_family at all. model_family says \"include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant\". The cert definitely covers many. The fragment's models: yes include.\n\nAlright, let me also decide attributes labels:\n- minimum_flow_q1 → \"minimum_flow_q1\"? Canonical: q1_minimum? The columns: \"Minimum Q1\", \"Transitional Q2\", \"Permanent Q3\", \"Overload Q4\", \"Ratio Q3/Q1\". Canonical labels: minimum_flow_q1, transitional_flow_q2, permanent_flow_q3, overload_flow_q4, ratio_q3_q1. Good.\n\nUnits: m³/h for the four flows. Ratio dimensionless (unit null).\n\nFootnote markers: none numbered. The bullet notes aren't footnotes with markers; I'll capture them in matrix_tables description (and maybe footnotes? No markers → skip footnotes array; or include as footnote with marker null? Schema requires marker; skip).\n\nThe \"Please note\" content: I'll include in the matrix_tables description for both tables (or a shared note). I'll put a concise description on each table entry.\n\nNow accuracy_class as attribute: since each model embeds class, no separate attribute needed. But maybe add attribute \"accuracy_class\" per model in model_family attributes. I'll include attributes 