ents, full bore, battery / renewable energy powered. Same flow-rate selection notes apply (Q2/Q1 = 1.6, Q4/Q3 = 1.25, Q3/Q1 at least 40, per OIML R49-1:2013 paragraph 4.1).\"\n\nAlso page mapping: table1 pages 7; table2 pages 8–9. Add \"pages\" key? Schema for matrix_tables doesn't include pages, but adding extra descriptive key might be OK — schema says name, columns, rows, description, footnotes (and example adds column_units). I'll include \"pages\" — hmm, rule-abiding: extra fields risk? The example itself added column_units, so extras allowed. I'll add pages for traceability. Actually keep it minimal: include \"pages\" — fine.\n\ntype_level: also maybe \"flow_rates_unit\": no. Skip.\n\nNow double-check the certificate \"scheme\": The number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — In NMi OIML-CS, format: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 where \"A\" after year... Historically NMi used \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" where A might denote the \"revision letter\"? Hmm. Actually NMi certificate numbers like \"R60/2000-NL1-11.01\" or \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"? I've seen \"A\" used in scheme A certs under OIML-CS (post-2017). Given 2013 recommendation but OIML-CS started 2017... this cert revision 8 with project 2563125 — plausibly issued under OIML-CS scheme A. The \"A\" in the number likely = scheme A. I'll include scheme \"A\". If wrong, minor.\n\nPage_total: 12 (from \"Page 7 of 12\").\n\nmember_state: \"The Netherlands\".\n\nShould I include \"oiml_issuer_id\"? Not present. Skip.\n\nNow assemble. I'll write JSON compactly but readably. Ensure valid JSON (no trailing commas). Use \"m³/h\" unicode superscript — the source uses m³/h; keep as is.\n\nOne more check — schema for characteristics structured values: each value must be object with value, unit_symbol, unit_id. For model_level entries, the example shows values: [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}] WITHOUT unit_symbol per value (unit at attribute level). I'll follow the example: attribute-level unit_symbol, value-level just model+value. But rule 2 says every characteristic value must be object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id... The R137 example contradicts (values are plain numbers with attribute-level unit_symbol). The example is authoritative for model_level format. I'll use {\"model\": ..., \"value\": ...} per R137 example. For type_level, use full structured objects {value, unit_symbol, unit_id}.\n\nHmm, but consistency... I'll follow the examples: type_level structured objects; model_level compact {\"model\",\"value\"}.\n\nFor footnote_markers in model values: none here.\n\nWrite out matrix_tables rows as objects per row.\n\nLet me now also think: should accuracy_class be numeric (1, 2) in rows or string (\"1\", \"1 & 2\")? String safer due to \"1 & 2\". Use strings \"1\", \"2\", \"1 & 2\".\n\nIn model_family model ids, use \"class 1\", \"class 2\", \"class 1 & 2\".\n\nAlright, compose final JSON.\n\nAlso: \"certified_type\": {\"category\": \"Water meter\"} — maybe also note \"family of instruments\" full bore... category only. Also add \"description\"? Could add: \"Family of water meters (full bore), battery / renewable energy powered variant\" — hmm, fragment-level. Keep category only... Actually adding a short description aids context: \"Water meters (R49); this fragment contains flow-rate tables for full-bore battery/renewable-energy-powered meters and a continuation table of another powering configuration.\" I'll add description — optional; include concise.\n\nLet me write it all.\n\nFinal check on model_level ordering: I'll keep consistent order across all 5 attributes (table A models then table B models).\n\nQ3 values list (57): \nA: 16000×19\nB: 40,40,63,63,100,160,250,250,630,630,1000,1000,1600,1600,2500,2500,4000,4000,6300,6300,6300,6300,10000,10000,10000,10000,10000,10000,16000,16000,16000,16000,16000,16000,16000,16000,16000\n\nWait — B Q3 recount from my row list: DN40:40,40; DN50:63,63 (4); DN65:100 (5); DN80:160 (6); DN100:250 (7); DN125:250 (8); DN150:630,630 (10); DN200:1000,1000 